{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793372025,"nonce":"AX4Tr2tWdzqi02QKB4wmlAwFcCU=","title":"Framework app previews: run OpenFL, HaxeFlixel, Heaps, Lime and Ceramic headless in the workspace and present them in RayDE","message":"The Ashui app preview (9ef9e94, 21132a7, 602e069) compiles an Ash/WASM artifact and runs it in an isolated browser frame bound to the tab. The other starters (Heaps, OpenFL, HaxeFlixel, Lime, Ceramic) have no preview: their Run builds and runs a native or JS build outside RayDE.\n\nIdea (darmie): intercept the framework's rendering, present it on RayDE's own Ashui surface, and forward input to the app, which runs headless in the workspace. One surface and input model for every framework, in the desktop app and in the browser, with the untrusted app kept in the workspace compute (MSB guest or local process), not in the editor.\n\n**Architecture**\n\nA common surface protocol and presentation, with a producer per framework:\n- Producer (inside the workspace, started through Procs like any program): a small shim library per framework captures the app's output and injects input. Talks the surface protocol over the process's stdio, so it works over a local process, a session program or a container the same way.\n- Transport: session relay of frames to the client and input back, bounded queues, latest-frame wins, connection-owned like the existing preview artifacts.\n- Presentation: an Ashui surface in the preview tab that draws the current frame scaled to the tab, captures pointer, keyboard, wheel, touch, focus and resize, and forwards them. Chrome (Rebuild, build progress, Output, errors) reuses AppPreview and the target-aware Run Configurations.\n\nProducer tiers, cheapest first; a framework may use more than one:\n1. JS build in the existing isolated preview frame (browser RayDE only): Heaps, OpenFL, Flixel and Lime build html5/JS, the frame already has the origin, headers and capability model. No interception, native fidelity. Not available to the desktop app, which has no browser frame.\n2. Pixel surface: headless render in the workspace, frames streamed. Works for every framework and both hosts. Costs bandwidth and needs software rendering in the guest.\n3. Display-list replay (OpenFL and Flixel only, later): serialize the scene (sprites, transforms, Graphics commands, bitmaps by resource id, text) and draw it with Ashui's DrawContext. Tiny bandwidth, resolution independent; limited by filters, shaders, blend modes and Stage3D. Heaps, with shader-based rendering, has no equivalent.\n\n**Open questions (each has a child issue)**\n- How a streamed frame becomes an Ashui surface: Bitmap is decode-and-display; a per-frame texture write needs a CanvasFrame paint hook or the media Player (asked of ashui).\n- Frame transport that fits a WAN: raw RGBA at 1280x720 is 3.7 MB a frame; needs damage rectangles, lossless tiles or a video codec.\n- Headless rendering in a guest with no GPU: Mesa software GL, SDL offscreen, or a framework software renderer.\n- Input fidelity: pointer capture, keyboard layouts and IME, focus, touch, gamepad.\n- Audio.\n\n**Done when** a Heaps and an OpenFL (or Flixel) starter project previews in the desktop app and in the browser against a hosted session, input works, the app is stopped and its resources released on tab close, and a crash or hang in the app shows in Output without taking RayDE down.\n\nRelated: 9ef9e94 (Ashui app preview tab), 03d642c (preview compiler, runtime and isolation contract), b50e457 (browser previews with remote sessions), ae3a156 (RayDE in the browser).","files":null}]}